WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.818
This is a CNC lathe up close.

2
00:00:02.818 --> 00:00:06.783
The chuck spins a steel shaft, and the tool stands still.

3
00:00:06.783 --> 00:00:11.166
It cuts with a small carbide insert, made of very hard carbide.

4
00:00:11.516 --> 00:00:16.919
The cutting speed is how fast the material slides past the cutting edge,

5
00:00:16.919 --> 00:00:18.495
in metres per minute.

6
00:00:18.495 --> 00:00:22.997
Carbide in steel runs at about 150 to 300 metres per minute.

7
00:00:24.197 --> 00:00:28.363
But the spindle turns in revolutions per minute.

8
00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:32.268
That spindle speed follows from the diameter.

9
00:00:32.268 --> 00:00:37.041
For a shaft of 60 millimetres at 220 metres per minute,

10
00:00:37.041 --> 00:00:40.946
one circumference is about 188.5 millimetres,

11
00:00:40.946 --> 00:00:45.372
so the spindle turns at 1,167 revolutions a minute.

12
00:00:45.722 --> 00:00:50.839
As the shaft gets thinner, the spindle must turn faster for the same cutting speed.

13
00:00:50.839 --> 00:00:55.462
That is why you hear a lathe speed up as the tool moves towards the centre.

14
00:00:56.662 --> 00:01:00.268
As the shaft turns, the tool slides along it.

15
00:01:00.268 --> 00:01:05.798
The feed is how far it moves on per revolution: here 0.3 millimetres.

16
00:01:06.148 --> 00:01:11.256
The depth of cut is how deep the tool goes in: you see it as the step in the shaft.

17
00:01:11.606 --> 00:01:16.296
Feed times spindle speed gives the feed rate:

18
00:01:16.296 --> 00:01:21.091
0.3 times 1,167 is 350 millimetres per minute.

19
00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:25.990
A cut of 200 millimetres then takes 34 seconds.

20
00:01:27.190 --> 00:01:29.048
Why not simply cut faster?

21
00:01:29.048 --> 00:01:32.120
Then the edge runs hotter and wears faster.

22
00:01:32.120 --> 00:01:34.621
How long it lasts is its tool life.

23
00:01:34.621 --> 00:01:39.122
Rule of thumb for carbide: 20 percent faster roughly halves it.

24
00:01:39.472 --> 00:01:43.798
So when roughing, engineers raise the feed first.

25
00:01:43.798 --> 00:01:50.419
At 0.4 it becomes 467 millimetres per minute, and the cut takes 26 seconds.

26
00:01:51.619 --> 00:01:53.576
Now look very closely.

27
00:01:53.576 --> 00:01:58.737
The tip of the insert is rounded: that is the nose radius,

28
00:01:58.737 --> 00:02:01.940
usually 0.4, 0.8 or 1.2 millimetres.

29
00:02:01.940 --> 00:02:04.609
It leaves a fine helix behind.

30
00:02:04.959 --> 00:02:07.885
The distance between the grooves is the feed.

31
00:02:07.885 --> 00:02:10.292
A larger radius makes them shallower.

32
00:02:10.292 --> 00:02:15.429
And the feed counts squared: halve it, and the roughness Ra drops to a quarter.

33
00:02:15.779 --> 00:02:24.326
With feed 0.25 and radius 0.8, the theoretical Ra is 2.4 micrometres.

34
00:02:24.326 --> 00:02:28.166
At feed 0.14, Ra drops to 0.77.

35
00:02:29.366 --> 00:02:31.971
The metal that comes off is the chip.

36
00:02:31.971 --> 00:02:35.210
The chip breaker, a small shape on the insert,

37
00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:39.083
curls it until it breaks: short curls, like a 6 or a 9.

38
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:42.528
With too little feed or depth of cut it does not work:

39
00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:45.853
then you get long, stringy chips that wrap round the part.

40
00:02:47.053 --> 00:02:50.562
A long, thin shaft bends away under the tool.

41
00:02:50.562 --> 00:02:55.942
What counts is its slenderness: the overhang divided by the diameter.

42
00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:59.451
Here 160 divided by 20: 8 times the diameter.

43
00:02:59.801 --> 00:03:01.854
Such a shaft starts to chatter.

44
00:03:01.854 --> 00:03:07.416
Above about 3 times the diameter, the tailstock supports the free end with a centre.

45
00:03:07.766 --> 00:03:13.838
Above about 6 times the diameter, a steady rest is added: a support with rollers halfway.

46
00:03:13.838 --> 00:03:15.544
So this shaft needs both.

47
00:03:16.743 --> 00:03:21.055
Finally, the turn-mill centre: a lathe that also mills and drills.

48
00:03:21.055 --> 00:03:26.216
Its turret carries driven tools: cutters and drills with their own small motor.

49
00:03:26.566 --> 00:03:30.021
The C-axis stops the spindle at a controlled angle:

50
00:03:30.021 --> 00:03:33.340
that is how you drill six holes 60 degrees apart.

51
00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.269
The Y-axis moves the tool off centre, for a straight flat.

52
00:03:37.619 --> 00:03:41.982
The part drops off finished: complete machining, in one set-up.

53
00:03:41.982 --> 00:03:47.800
A flange that took 4.0 plus 3.0 minutes on two machines is done here in 6.0 minutes.

54
00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:53.227
And a bar feeder keeps pushing a bar of about 3 metres a little further in.

55
00:03:53.227 --> 00:03:56.206
So it runs on unmanned after the last shift.
